/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fix a case to a vibrating surface.
   - m is the seismic mass in kg; c is the damping coefficient in N·s/m; k is the spring constant in N/m; z is the relative displacement in m; y is the case displacement in m; t is time in s.
   - Divide by m and write the standard second-order form.
   - H = Z ωn² / A is the normalised amplitude ratio: the relative displacement Z per unit case acceleration A, multiplied by ωn² so that H = 1 at low frequency; D = √((1 − r²)² + (2ζr)²) is the denominator, so H = 1/D; r = f/fn is dimensionless
   - The relative displacement amplitude is Z = (A/ωn²) H, where A is the case acceleration amplitude in m/s².
   - An accelerometer is a seismic transducer used with f ≪ fn, usually r below about 0.2 to 0.4 (with ζ = 0.7, H stays within 1 % of 1 up to r ≈ 0.4).
   - Damping is chosen near ζ = 0.6 to 0.7.
   - Mounting matters.
   - A seismometer uses the other end of the curve.
   - In a car airbag, a MEMS accelerometer detects the sudden deceleration of a crash and the electronic control unit fires the bag within milliseconds.
*/
