/* book exam traps: Using an accelerometer at or near its natural frequency: H becomes large and the phase shifts, so the waveform is wrong even if the sensor does not break. | Forgetting the mounting resonance: a stud, glue or magnet can add a peak inside the measurement band. | Mixing the two ratios: H = 1/D is the acceleration response; Z/Y = r²/D is the displacement response used by a seismometer. | Dropping units in fn = (1/2π)√(k/m): use k in N/m and m in kg to get Hz; grams or N/mm give a wrong frequency. | Thinking zero damping is best: low ζ gives a sharp resonance peak and a large phase change; about ζ = 0.7 is often chosen. */
