/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a thin rectangular plate of n-type semiconductor.
   - Charge piles up on that edge until the electric field it creates pushes back with equal force.
   - VH is the Hall voltage in volts; RH is the Hall coefficient in m³/C; I is the control current in amperes; B is the flux density in tesla; t is the plate thickness in metres.
   - n is the carrier density in carriers per m³; e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C is the electron charge.
   - Two design facts follow.
   - The plate is driven by a constant current, not a constant voltage.
   - A practical sensor includes more than the plate.
   - The amplifier itself is treated with signal conditioning (differential and instrumentation amplifiers).
   - A Hall current sensor wraps a ferrite ring around the conductor and cuts a narrow air gap of length g into the ring.
   - B is the gap flux density in tesla; μ0 = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H/m; I is the conductor current in amperes; g is the gap length in metres.
*/
