/* book exam traps: Do not confuse the Hall coefficient RH (m³/C, a material property) with the sensitivity in V/T: sensitivity is RH I / t and also depends on current and thickness. | Thickness must be in metres. Leaving 0.1 mm as 0.1 in the formula gives an answer 1000 times too small. | For p-type material the Hall coefficient is positive, so the Hall voltage polarity reverses compared with n-type at the same current and field. Examiners like this sign question. | The Hall voltage appears across the two side contacts, perpendicular to both the current and the field. On the wrong pair of edges the Hall component is zero, but you still read the resistive IR drop of the plate, which is usually much larger than VH. */
